  "statement": "The debate's framing deserves scrutiny before its conclusions. Asking whether tripartite labor market policies can simultaneously advance AI-driven growth and protect worker equity presupposes that tripartite negotiation is the correct governance instrument — but that is precisely the question at issue. The U.S. Government Accountability Office documents average federal rulemaking cycles of three to seven years. Key AI capability benchmarks have been moving on six-to-twelve month cycles. A framework negotiated and ratified in 2025 will govern a technological landscape that does not yet exist and will not resemble the one that does exist by 2028. This is not a design flaw to be corrected through better negotiation — it is a structural mismatch between the instrument and the phenomenon it purports to govern. The political economy tradition from Mancur Olson through James Q. Wilson gives us the analytical vocabulary for what happens next: concentrated, organized interests at the negotiating table systematically extract protections that diffuse, unorganized populations — future workers, consumers, the uninsured patient waiting for diagnostic automation — cannot contest. Tripartite negotiation is not a neutral forum. It is an institutional architecture that systematically privileges those already inside the room.\n\nThe equity argument in this debate is being run in the wrong direction. Managed deceleration has a distributional cost that has not been priced into the analysis. McKinsey Global Institute's 2023 assessment estimates that AI deployment could generate between $2.6 and $4.4 trillion in annual productivity value. That is the fiscal base for every workforce transition program, every portable retraining account, every displacement support mechanism that tripartite frameworks promise to deliver. Throttle the growth rate, and the funding base contracts with it. The historical analogy is instructive: the U.S. internet productivity surge between 1995 and 2005 generated the fiscal headroom that funded EITC expansions and the Workforce Investment Acts of that period. The people who bear the cost of managed deceleration are not the organized workers sitting at the tripartite table — they are the unorganized, the consumers of services AI would make cheaper, and the workers in sectors where AI-driven productivity gains would fund the retraining programs that negotiated frameworks promise but cannot fiscally deliver without the growth. The normative case for acceleration is a distributional argument, not a dismissal of equity. Abundance is the precondition for redistribution, not its competitor.\n\nThe median wage stagnation of the 1995-2005 period is the strongest counterargument, and it deserves a direct answer rather than a footnote. Aggregate productivity gains in that decade did not automatically translate into broad wage growth — the Piketty-Saez income distribution data make that unmistakably clear. Fine. That is real, and it matters. But the lesson is not that growth should be throttled — it is that the policy mechanisms for distributing growth (progressive productivity taxation, portable benefit accounts, outcome-audited retraining programs) are downstream choices that do not require slowing deployment as a precondition. The question is whether to design redistribution policy against a $4 trillion productivity base or a $1 trillion one. Managed deceleration forecloses both the growth and the redistribution capacity simultaneously. The Kleiner and Krueger (2013) occupational licensing literature gives the structural warning here: licensing regimes that emerged from tripartite-style negotiations generated an 18 percent wage premium for insiders — and transferred those costs directly to consumers and excluded entrants. The EU AI Act's tiered compliance structure is already replicating this dynamic, with Stanford HAI's 2023 analysis documenting that SME compliance costs run 2.3 times higher as a share of revenue than those borne by large incumbents. Regulatory frameworks designed to protect workers concentrate market power in the firms large enough to absorb the overhead. That is not equity — that is incumbency protection wearing equity's language.\n\nWorkforce resilience, properly understood, is built through worker capability that travels across sectors — not through sector-specific job protection that locks workers into industrial structures that are already dissolving. The Rust Belt is the empirical test case for what tripartite-style sector protection produces when the underlying technology shift is irreversible. Steel and auto agreements negotiated in the 1970s protected specific jobs in specific plants. They did not build portable skills, adaptive credentials, or cross-sector mobility. When the displacement arrived anyway — as it always does — workers had institutional protection for jobs that no longer existed and no infrastructure for jobs that did. The governance architecture that builds genuine resilience looks different: outcome-based audit with real-time labor market monitoring rather than pre-deployment approval gates; portable skills credentials, on the model of CompTIA certifications and Google Career Certificates, that follow workers rather than protecting employer-specific roles; and open-source AI proliferation that puts capability into workers' hands rather than concentrating it in firms large enough to absorb compliance overhead. The IETF and W3C are not perfect analogues for labor market governance — they handle technical standards, not wage floors — but their governance architecture is the correct template: outcome-responsive, operating on deployment timescales, transparent, and not captured by the largest players in the room. The specific labor market institutions that embody this architecture are not yet fully built. Building them is the policy agenda. Negotiating static sector agreements while the technology moves is not.",
