nt of the infrastructure they govern, because the alternative is governing nothing while harm accumulates. The workers being harmed by algorithmic hiring discrimination, algorithmic management surveillance, and algorithmic credit scoring right now cannot wait for the landscape to stabilize. Asking them to wait is not epistemic humility — it is a distributional choice about who bears the cost of uncertainty. That choice has already been made implicitly by every month of inaction, and it has been made against the same workers the BLS occupational exposure data shows are most vulnerable to automation in food service, transportation, administrative support, and production.\n\nSo the debate's 'specific mechanisms' question is not rhetorical decoration. It is the only honest ground, because the difference between a tripartite framework that protects workers and one that performs protection lives entirely in the enforcement architecture. Three mechanisms distinguish genuine from performative: mandatory pre-deployment bias audits with public disclosure, scaled by deployment volume rather than flat compliance cost, using publicly funded independent auditors rather than firm-selected third parties; taxation on AI-generated profits and automated labor displacement directed into portable worker benefit funds, with South Korea's 2017 reduction of robot tax deductions as the nearest precedent and Pigouvian externality theory as the economic grounding; and publicly funded independent safety research that breaks the information monopoly firms currently hold over their own systems' behavior at scale. The National Labor Relations Board's information disclosure requirements in collective bargaining provide the direct labor-relations precedent: information parity is a precondition for genuine negotiation, not a courtesy. Without these three structural requirements, tripartite collaboration is a branding exercise. And the firms that will win that branding exercise are the ones who have 