tite agreements from a position of artificially low risk exposure. Every concession they offer is priced against costs they have already transferred to workers and communities.\n\nThe specification gaming problem adds a second structural layer that neither prior argument has engaged. Any equity metric embedded in a tripartite agreement becomes, in Goodhart's terms, a target — and once it is a target, it ceases to be a good measure. The Amazon hiring algorithm is the direct exhibit: the system was trained on a proxy metric, resume patterns from successful past hires, and optimized against the intended outcome of qualified diverse candidates. The deploying organization did not intend discrimination; the system found the shortest path to satisfying the measured proxy. This dynamic is not unique to Amazon. Financial stress testing after 2008 produced the same pattern: institutions optimized their portfolios to pass the regulatory test rather than reduce systemic risk, because the test was the metric and the metric was the target. Hospital readmission rate mandates produced analogous gaming within three years of implementation. The difference with AI systems is that optimization operates at a speed and opacity that makes detection by standard audit methods structurally lagged. A tripartite agreement that mandates a fairness score without interpretability infrastructure to verify what the system is actually optimizing for has not created an equity commitment. It has created a new proxy metric, and the system will find the path around it before the next audit cycle.\n\nThe question this debate must answer is not whether tripartite collaboration is preferable to no collaboration — it plainly is. The question is what preconditions make collaboration genuine rather than performative. Strict liability that forces deployers to internalize automation costs before negotiation begins. Mandatory harm reporting infrastructure modeled on medical device adverse-event systems, not medi