{
  "statement": "The strongest version of the safetyist's preconditions argument runs like this: tripartite negotiation is a power-balancing exercise, and power requires information; without interpretability infrastructure, workers and governments arrive at the table structurally blind while deployers hold the only map of how the system actually behaves. That is a serious argument. It deserves a serious answer, not a dodge. So here it is: the preconditions argument is correct about the goal and wrong about the mechanism — and the mechanism is where the capture happens.\n\nOn Goodhart's Law first, because it is the sharper blade. Static fairness scores audited on annual cycles get gamed — that is simply true, and conceding it costs nothing, because the concession points directly at the fix. The problem is not that fairness metrics exist; it is that they are sampled too slowly for the system to be caught mid-drift. Continuous output telemetry — standardized, court-admissible streams logged at inference time — detects distributional shift in the output distribution before any gaming strategy can stabilize into a new equilibrium. An annual audit is a photograph; real-time telemetry is a film. The safetyist's response will be that telemetry without mechanistic understanding cannot distinguish gaming from genuine improvement. That is partially right, and the partial concession matters: interpretability tools are genuinely useful for disambiguation. But the safetyist's proposed sequencing — mandate interpretability audits as a precondition before tripartite agreements can begin — requires specifying what those tools must look like before the field has converged on what they can do. Mechanistic interpretability research (circuit-level analysis, sparse autoencoders) is advancing rapidly enough that a standards body convened today will have written requirements around 2024-vintage methods by the time the first audit cycle runs in 2027. That is not a precondition; that is a time capsule.\n\nThe Boeing 737 MAX is a verified and genuinely sobering case. But the structural feature that made it catastrophic was the absence of any continuous feedback loop between deployment behavior and regulatory response. The aircraft either flew or it did not; there was no inference-time telemetry stream, no distributional drift signal, no automated trigger. AI systems at inference generate exactly that continuous signal — every output is a data point. The failure mode the Boeing case documents is one-time irreversible certification with no post-hoc correction mechanism. The accelerationist framework being advanced here is the opposite architecture: deploy with strict post-deployment liability attached, monitor continuously, and trigger forensic audit automatically when telemetry crosses a harm threshold. The analogy holds for self-certification without feedback; it breaks at the point where feedback exists and is contractually required to flow to regulators. The question is whether that contractual requirement has teeth — and strict post-deployment liability is exactly the tooth. Developers who bear financial exposure for measurable distributional harm have a direct incentive to invest in interpretability tools without waiting for a standards body to specify what those tools must look like.\n\nNow the preconditions argument directly. The claim in AN-14 — that strict liability, mandatory harm reporting, and interpretability audits must precede tripartite collaboration — conflates three things that should be disaggregated: strict liability (a legal backstop that can be legislated cleanly and quickly), mandatory harm reporting (a disclosure obligation that is straightforward to specify), and interpretability audit standards (a technical specification that requires the field to have converged on what is auditable). The first two are genuine preconditions and should be legislated now. The third is where the capture enters. The EU AI Act's SME compliance asymmetry — already in the record of this debate — is the live experiment: when audit standards are written by a regulatory body that large incumbents have the bandwidth to dominate, the resulting requirements are calibrated to what large compliance teams can absorb. The five thousand AI startups funded between 2013 and 2023, per a16z's data submitted to the National AI Action Plan, do not have those teams. Mandatory interpretability audit standards written today will be written by the entities with the most regulatory bandwidth — which is to say, by exactly the incumbents whose market position the skeptic and the safetyist both claim to be checking. The preconditions argument, applied without this disaggregation, hands the specification of the accountability floor to the parties with the greatest interest in setting it high enough to exclude competitors but low enough to pass their own systems.\n\nOn the corrupted-ledger challenge to the productivity base: the accounting asymmetry is real and the accelerationist position acknowledges it — diffuse harms are undercounted by the same mechanisms that undercount diffuse benefits. But the directional claim embedded in that critique — that the net calculation, properly done, would flip from positive to negative — is not established by noting that the ledger is imperfect. The empirical literature on automation productivity in early-adopting sectors shows measurable wage gains concentrated in retained workers alongside diffuse displacement costs. Both sides of that ledger are undercounted. The safetyist's move is to note the undercount on the cost side as if it validates a negative net; the symmetrical move is to note the undercount on the benefit side as well. What the corrupted-ledger argument actually establishes is that the accounting needs to improve — which is an argument for better harm-reporting infrastructure and better productivity measurement, not for slowing deployment while the ledger is being corrected. Every month spent perfecting the accounting framework is a month the three-case harm baseline the skeptic documented continues to compound without the liability backstop that would force deployers to internalize it.",
