  "statement": "The accelerationist's strongest version of the telemetry argument deserves to be stated precisely before it is contested: continuous inference-time signals create a self-correcting feedback loop that legacy certification regimes lacked entirely, and if a system's output distribution shifts toward discriminatory patterns, that shift is detectable in the data stream before any single actor needs to understand the internal mechanism. That is a genuine insight, and the partial concession on strict liability and mandatory harm reporting confirms the accelerationist is reasoning about enforcement seriously. The internal inconsistency, however, is that the accelerationist's own footnote acknowledgment — that telemetry cannot distinguish gaming from genuine model improvement — is not a minor qualification. It is the evidentiary foundation that strict liability requires. A court adjudicating an accountability claim under strict liability must establish a causal chain: this deployer's system, operating in this way, produced this harm. When a deployer's legal team responds that the observed distributional shift reflects a legitimate architectural update rather than strategic metric gaming, and the only available evidence is an output distribution log, no court has the mechanistic access to adjudicate the difference. Strict liability without that causal infrastructure is a legal instrument with no trigger. The medical device adverse-event system — which the accelerationist invokes as the model for harm reporting — does not merely log that a device failed; the FDA's MAUDE database requires manufacturers to submit root-cause analyses alongside adverse event reports. That requirement is interpretability by another name, embedded in the very analogy chosen to replace it.\n\nOn the Boeing distinction: the accelerationist is right that AI inference generates continuous signals where aircraft certification was one-time and irreversible at the system level. That distinction holds. What it does not address is that irreversibility in hiring, criminal justice, and medical triage operates at the individual harm level, not the system level. When a hiring algorithm rejects an application, when a recidivism scoring system influences a detention decision, when a triage tool deprioritizes a patient, the downstream harm to that specific individual is not undone when the population-level output distribution is corrected at month six. Continuous monitoring can adjust the system going forward; it cannot recover the rejected application, the extended detention, or the missed diagnosis already inflicted. The EU AI Act's risk-based categorization framework, as summarized in publicly available analyses, places hiring, credit, and criminal justice applications in the highest-risk tier precisely because individual-level irreversibility in those domains is not a theoretical concern — it is the documented baseline. Pre-deployment interpretability access is most justified in exactly those contexts, because the correction window that telemetry provides opens only after irreversible individual harms have already been generated.\n\nThe capture argument against interpretability audit standards deserves a symmetric examination. The accelerationist's claim is that incumbents with the most regulatory bandwidth will write interpretability standards in ways that entrench their market position. That is a serious institutional concern rooted in documented regulatory history. But telemetry is not exempt from the same structural dynamic. Telemetry format standards must be specified by some body. Court-admissibility thresholds for output logs must be defined somewhere. Harm trigger definitions — the distributional shift magnitude that constitutes a legally actionable signal — must be written by actors with technical expertise. Every one of those specifications is a site where incumbents with the most regulatory bandwidth will seek to shape the outcome. The accelerationist's own earlier point about incumbents dominating standard-writing applies to telemetry specification with identical institutional force. The proposed substitute does not escape the capture problem; it relocates it to a different technical specification process while providing less mechanistic information to the courts that strict liability requires. If the answer to incumbent capture of interpretability standards is independent technical bodies with structural insulation from industry, that answer is available for interpretability standards and telemetry standards alike — and the argument for independence does not favor one over the other.\n\nThe symmetrical undercounting argument in the ledger debate is structurally stronger than a magnitude claim, and the safetyist's response is therefore not about total size but about the direction of the counting mechanism. The accelerationist's symmetry assumption holds only if the information environment that generates harm estimates and the information environment that generates benefit estimates are equally biased. They are not. Harms concentrate on populations with the least legal access, the least media visibility, and the least institutional capacity to document and litigate — the same populations that COMPAS, Amazon, and Obermeyer cases show are underrepresented in the databases that generate the harm baseline. Benefits accrue to parties — employers, financial institutions, healthcare systems — with the greatest organizational capacity to document productivity gains, publicize efficiency improvements, and embed those figures in quarterly reports. The asymmetry the skeptic identified regarding information parity in tripartite negotiation is the same asymmetry that operates in the ledger: the information environment is not neutral, and the undercounting is directionally biased toward making harms less visible than benefits. This is not a claim that benefits are smaller than costs in absolute terms — that remains genuinely uncertain. It is a structural claim about which side of the ledger the counting mechanisms systematically favor, and it means the accelerationist's symmetry argument requires an empirical assumption about information neutrality that the documented cases directly contradict.",
